brief_summary:
The goal of this randomized controlled trial is to learn whether the format in which a worked clinical case is presented affects learning in second-, third- and fourth-year Nutrition and Dietetics students. The same case, the nutritional assessment of a young adult who recently adopted a vegan diet in five decision steps, is presented as a narrated video, a web-based gamified case, or a written worked case.

The main questions it aims to answer are:

* Do students who study the video-based or the gamified case score higher on a knowledge test immediately after the session than students who study the text-based worked case?
* Are knowledge differences maintained 4 weeks later, and do clinical reasoning, satisfaction and self-confidence in learning differ between groups? Researchers will compare the video-based and gamified groups with the text-based worked case group (control).

Participants will:

* complete a sociodemographic form, a 12-item knowledge test and the Clinical Reasoning Scale before the session;
* study the material of their randomly assigned group individually, for up to 15 minutes, in a single class session;
* complete a parallel knowledge test, the Clinical Reasoning Scale and the Student Satisfaction and Self-Confidence in Learning Scale immediately afterwards;
* complete a third parallel knowledge test and the Clinical Reasoning Scale 4 weeks (±3 days) later.
